y }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/06c51ecc423683f4377248004b7a318f1348836bd752a7f65f94457d1774e64d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}2\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "abaa9270682b58d8b9e9a28aaa831c88fe855525b815e382ccfdc43dfe25035a"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-152875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-351513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-351513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}}{\\sqrt{3}}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/1c0fc31d392bbaccf9a292553321bd60ccbdb2a02f3675543797f570c4fa0ef1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-351513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "abaa9270682b58d8b9e9a28aaa831c88fe855525b815e382ccfdc43dfe25035a"} {"question" "q-351513" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-351513" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-20891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-20891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}}{\\sqrt{2}}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/5e9932629e5a201c2ada61157fdfa39351962a55116e2980157b1823907159f2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-20891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "abaa9270682b58d8b9e9a28aaa831c88fe855525b815e382ccfdc43dfe25035a"} {"question" "q-20891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-20891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-20891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-152417" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{1-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-152417" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=1-{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2x\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1-{x}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{1-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{1-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{2}^{u}]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{a},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{2}^{a}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/15f110a3a228573a5ec349664615293be38fff8c4bd4f14a8ec1b11b997cb818.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{1-{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-152417" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "abaa9270682b58d8b9e9a28aaa831c88fe855525b815e382ccfdc43dfe25035a"} {"question" "q-152417" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-152417" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-152417" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-351523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-351523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{4}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{4}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/e50ca6e9326f5a6146a7a486adcc2d3d5f5c059eae3e2e1b06fbff8664b1c7df.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-351523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "abaa9270682b58d8b9e9a28aaa831c88fe855525b815e382ccfdc43dfe25035a"} {"question" "q-351523" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-351523" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\ln(4)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-351539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-351539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{3u-1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{3u-1}}{3}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-1}-{e}^{3a-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{3x-1}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x-1}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{3x-1}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/6a5fd63a29d7c8f5fba712c7fa6c20620c9a1438750f3197606e726a85666827.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x-1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-351539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "abaa9270682b58d8b9e9a28aaa831c88fe855525b815e382ccfdc43dfe25035a"} {"question" "q-351539" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "q-351539" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3e}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/state.json" "file_sha256" "72467d1772803c4f69cd2ae61bea7112854ba56bd76303be3cadbb62f44fdaa4"} {"question" "e-10554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^\\infty   e^{-3x}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/example-10554.json" "file_sha256" "a4b7998581fef8b80a92bf1d79b4ae0fec79ca69a99fbe4506043f668d4ad30c"} {"question" "e-10554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\,]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/bc4ffc41105491f2bd6b37b2482afc64946d8f67f6db83dfa322c400ae4bc2b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/example-10554.json" "file_sha256" "a4b7998581fef8b80a92bf1d79b4ae0fec79ca69a99fbe4506043f668d4ad30c"} {"question" "e-10764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}  3\\cdot 2^{2x+1}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/example-10764.json" "file_sha256" "8ac2b85adcb57ae8f2c3d70ca3bd248242564bfea3185ab496ccfcdc58425411"} {"question" "e-10764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{2x+1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{0}-{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/009f467677932d0ac17e1632c31f4d3aa5271ffe332730d9670e69862b243bef.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/example-10764.json" "file_sha256" "8ac2b85adcb57ae8f2c3d70ca3bd248242564bfea3185ab496ccfcdc58425411"} {"question" "e-10553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty  x^3\\cdot 2^{-x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/example-10553.json" "file_sha256" "a8b73808a15e1cb7a9cd70ced2fd54a137d75761ce0fa4faa15baba6b373a9ff"} {"question" "e-10553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/example-10553.json" "file_sha256" "a8b73808a15e1cb7a9cd70ced2fd54a137d75761ce0fa4faa15baba6b373a9ff"}] "usage" {"e-10553" [] "e-10764" [] "q-20891" [] "e-10554" []} "basis" 1335 "retractions" [[17592186193070 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $∫_{0}^{∞}\\frac{e^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}dx$."] [17592186192257 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^\\infty e^{-3x} \\,\\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186192257 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{∞}e^{-3x}dx &= lim_(a → ∞)∫_{-1}^{a}e^{-3x}dx \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)[- \\frac{e^{-3x}}{3}]_{-1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[e^{-3x}]_{-1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)e^{-3a} - e^{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4004/Source/Images/121e8b397fd3d1ba5e806dfe5ca38245.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{∞}e^{-3x} &= -\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)e^{-3a} - e^{3}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (0 - e^{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{3}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192292 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} 3\\cdot 2^{2x+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186192292 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{- ∞}^{0}3 \\cdot 2^{2x + 1}dx &= 3 \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)∫_{a}^{0}2^{2x + 1}dx \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)[(2^{2x + 1})/(2\\ln (2))]_{a}^{0} \\\\\n&= (3 \\cdot 2^{1})/(2\\ln (2)) \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)[2^{2x}]_{a}^{0} \\\\\n&= (3)/(\\ln (2)) \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)(2^{0} - 2^{2a}) \\\\\n&= (3)/(\\ln (2)) \\cdot (1 - lim_(a → - ∞)2^{2a})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4004/Source/Images/e9da045e68ed322bd54138b5cd02b995.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{- ∞}^{0}3 \\cdot 2^{2x + 1}dx &= (3)/(\\ln (2)) \\cdot (1 - lim_(a → - ∞)2^{2a}) \\\\\n&= (3)/(\\ln (2)) \\cdot (1 - 0) \\\\\n&= (3)/(\\ln (2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192256 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty x^3\\cdot 2^{-x^4} \\,\\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186192256 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x^4$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty$, the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{∞}x^{3} \\cdot 2^{-x^{4}}dx &= \\frac{1}{4}∫_{1}^{∞}2^{-u}du\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{∞}x^{3} \\cdot 2^{-x^{4}}dx &= \\frac{1}{4}∫_{1}^{∞}2^{-u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)∫_{1}^{a}2^{-u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[- \\frac{2^{-u}}{\\ln 2}]_{1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[2^{-u}]_{1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)\\frac{1}{2^{a}} - \\frac{1}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2^x}\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4004/Source/Images/769170756b4db689d820f57e3f8d9791.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{∞}x^{3} \\cdot 2^{-x^{4}}dx &= -\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)\\frac{1}{2^{a}} - \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot (0 - \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8\\ln 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186191192 17592186191193 17592186191194 17592186191195 17592186191196] "immutable_fields" [[17592186191191 :answer-field/correct] [17592186191191 :answer-field/id] [17592186191191 :answer-field/key] [17592186191191 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
